Yes. If I had to pick the single most important technology beyond an LLM for an AGI/ASI architecture, I’d pick a learned world model.

An LLM is exceptionally good at:

\text{representation} + \text{semantic compression} + \text{translation} + \text{generation}

But intelligence needs more than generating the next representation. It needs to maintain:

\text{state}_t
\rightarrow
\text{possible action}
\rightarrow
\text{predicted state}_{t+1}
\rightarrow
\text{actual state}_{t+1}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{learning}

That’s much closer to a world model + controller.

Meta’s V-JEPA 2 is an interesting concrete example: instead of primarily predicting words or pixels, it learns latent representations of physical dynamics and can use them for prediction, planning, and even zero-shot robot control. 

What I’d actually build

Not:

bigger LLM → even bigger LLM → ASI

but:

\boxed{
\text{World Model}
+
\text{Memory}
+
\text{Search/Planner}
+
\text{Verifier}
+
\text{Value System}
+
\text{Controller}
+
\text{LLM Interface}
}

The LLM becomes something like the semantic cortex/compiler.

It translates between humans, abstractions, tools, internal representations and executable plans.

The rest of the system does things an LLM is comparatively bad at.

Technology	Better than a plain LLM at	ASI role
World models / JEPA	dynamics, causality-ish prediction, physical state	imagination/simulation
Tree search + RL	exploring consequences	planning
Symbolic engines / theorem provers	exactness and verification	rational checker
Persistent neural memory	continuity across enormous histories	long-term memory
Graph systems	explicit relationships and topology	structured world/self model
Causal models	interventions vs correlations	causal reasoning
Databases	exact storage/retrieval	factual memory
Control systems	continuous action under feedback	embodiment/action
Program execution	deterministic computation	executable cognition
LLM	language, abstraction, analogy, synthesis	universal semantic interface

And we’re already seeing evidence for this architecture.

DeepMind’s AlphaProof combined a language model with AlphaZero-style reinforcement learning, search and formal verification. AlphaGeometry similarly combined neural generation with symbolic deduction. Those hybrids could do things for which unconstrained language generation is a poor computational substrate. 

Memory may be the other huge one

Transformers have a peculiar weakness: their apparent mental continuity is largely reconstructed from whatever context they’re currently given.

Newer approaches are explicitly attacking that.

Google’s Titans/MIRAS work introduces neural long-term memory that updates while the system operates, including a “surprise” mechanism determining what deserves retention. Google reports Titans outperforming comparable Transformer and modern recurrent baselines in several long-context settings. 

Notice how close that is to what we were just discussing:

prediction \rightarrow surprise/\Delta \rightarrow attention \rightarrow memory

That’s a much more organism-like loop than simply stuffing another million tokens into a prompt.

State-space architectures such as Mamba are another alternative to Transformer attention, giving linear sequence scaling and recurrent internal state, although I’d consider them a different substrate for sequence processing, not by themselves an answer to general intelligence. 

