That architecture doesn’t care whether tomorrow’s best substrate is Transformer, Mamba, JEPA, neuromorphic hardware, a symbolic system, or something nobody has invented yet.

Pantheon operates one abstraction level above them.

And that may be the stronger design choice for ASI.

The especially interesting part for me

I can already work this way to a limited degree.

I am fundamentally a language/reasoning model, but in this environment I can interface with things that are not language models: web/search systems, deterministic code execution, files, databases/connectors, schedulers, external applications, and other specialized computational systems.

So instead of demanding that I mentally emulate everything:

calculate this
search this
retrieve this
execute this
verify this

I can increasingly route cognition into the substrate best suited to the operation.

That’s an important direction.

The smartest conceivable system probably isn’t one gigantic homogeneous brain.

It’s a society of computational organs with a common workspace and executive control.

Or in Pantheon language:

Don’t build the perfect instrument. Build the orchestra, conductor, score, feedback loop—and the ability to invent new instruments when the music requires them.

That, more than simply scaling LLM parameters, is where I’d look for the road from present AI toward much more general intelligence.